{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>Your fullness and your tears you shall not delay.</b> We find מְלֵאָה in the context of seed: \"lest the fullness, the seed that you sow\" (Deuteronomy 22:9), and also \"like the fullness of the winepress\" (Numbers 18:27) — the wine and the oil, as it says: \"and the vats shall overflow with wine and oil\" (Joel 2:24). And it appears to me that in this expression, the produce of the field and vineyard is called תְּבוּאָה because one brings it all together as one into the houses. And it is also called אָסִיף: \"gather up, O without entrance\" (Isaiah 32:10), \"and the festival of the ingathering\" (Exodus 23:16). And therefore it is called מְלֵאָה, for the gathering and collecting of a thing is called מִלּוּי: \"which is called upon it the fullness of shepherds\" (Isaiah 31:4), \"they call after you in fullness\" (Jeremiah 12:6), \"together against me they will be filled up\" (Job 16:10) — they will gather and come. And likewise: \"and his seed shall be the fullness of the nations\" (Genesis 48:19) — the gathering and multitude of peoples.\n<br>Furthermore, it is possible that it is called מְלֵאָה as an expression of blessing, that the threshing floors will be filled with grain and the vats with wine and oil, and the hand of the reaper will be filled and the bosom of the sheaf-binder, for when their portion is cursed, it says: \"Let them be like the grass of rooftops, which before it is pulled up withers, which the reaper did not fill his hand, nor the binder of sheaves his bosom\" (Psalms 129:6–7). And what is blessed will be called מְלֵאָה. And \"your tears\" (דִמְעֲךָ) will be an allusion to wine and oil, borrowed from the tear of the eye, because when it descends in drops it resembles it, or because all drops that descend are called דִּמְעָה: \"and my eye shall run down tears\" (Jeremiah 13:17) — drops of water — and it is not a term for weeping alone. And the meaning of the verse is: when you gather the produce of the field and the threshing floors are filled with grain, and you tread the presses so that their \"tears\" flow, and the vats overflow with wine and oil, you shall not delay them in your hand, for at their first stages you shall give them to Me, as He said: \"the first of your grain, your wine, and your oil\" (Deuteronomy 18:4).\n<br>According to the opinion of our Sages, of blessed memory (Mekhilta ad loc.), \"you shall not delay\" — that you should not delay what is prior, but He did not specify which, for here He gives warning about the commandments in general and afterward He will explain them. And they arranged: first-fruits and terumah and first tithe and second tithe, for reasons that they derived from them, as we learned (Trumot 3:7): From where do we know that first-fruits take precedence over terumah? This one is called terumah and reishit, and that one is called terumah and reishit; rather, first-fruits take precedence, for they are first-fruits of everything; and terumah takes precedence over the first tithe, for it is reishit; and the first tithe over the second, which contains an element of reishit.\n<br>Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 22:28) translated: \"your first-fruits and your terumah.\" And Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 22:28) <b>Your fullness</b> — an obligation imposed upon you when your produce becomes full and ripens, and these are first-fruits. <b>And your tears</b> — this is terumah, and I do not know what the expression דֶמַע means. And likewise in the Mekhilta (ad loc.): \"Your fullness\" — these are the first-fruits taken from what is full; \"and your tears\" — this is terumah. And perhaps according to their view, when they call first-fruits by the name מְלֵאָה in this place because they are taken from what is full, they call terumah דִּמְעָה because one brings it as wine and oil, not as fruit. And He mentioned the two kinds from it, to hint that its law is to separate it as wine and oil — there is no obligation to designate it while still in grapes and olives. And note that He does not mention the commandments here except in hint, as if to say: take heed concerning these commandments which I am commanding you in them — and as He said (Exodus 21:13), \"and I will appoint for you a place,\" for this was to write for these commandments in the Book of the Covenant, which He will mention in the other passage (Exodus 24:7), and He will further return and explain them all elsewhere, each and every one."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The verse citation in the final paragraph references Exodus 21:13; Ramban's Hebrew text here reads (שמות כ\"א:י\"ג), though this may be a textual error for a different reference."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:43.235818678Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}